This is my first blog post. The topic of today is Epistomology. 

Why is it relevant : The way we look at the world defines "us" in a unique way. Tobe more precise:we have a mental model of the world that forms the basis of our actions in the world. 
In fact I would go far as to say that is what distinguishes us from each other more than external appearances or race. Acquiring knowledge is not so much about knowing facts; but about forming a mental model.
In fact in some Indian traditions: knowledge is considered to be structre of one's consciousness. It is important for one to have a proper framework: both to develop and understanding of oursleves and also of the world.

At the outset everyone has their own truth: we all have evolved under different circumstances with different experiences so our truth's are not all the same. Yet there is shared knowledge- within a community or a species.
There is also universal knowledge: common models we share as sentient beings, as well as apriori knowlege: things we come hardwired with. And although each of our truths are different : there is right knowledge and wrong knowledge.
These concepts though seemingly opposite, can live with each other if we recognize the fact that one person's knowledge is a subset of the overall universal pool of knowledge. So there are commonaities and differences.
Right knowledge is consistent with all existing knowledge and observations. An experience either fits in with our model of reality: in which case the model is reinforced and strengthened, contrary to our model, or outside our model. 
In the latter two cases we have to update our model to suit the new facts : and again we are in Right knowledge.

Wrong Knowledge is living in contradictions. I will admit a certain dissonance and I think all of us have a certain dissonance - as we are work in progress...but recognizing our blind spots is a good beginning to better knowledge.
In Indian philosophy testimony is a valid means to acquire knowledge. We take Einstien's word for theory of relativity- we don't need to examine the "why" of everything we learn to learn it. We trust authority, as long as we dont see a contradiction with experience/experiment.
If there is we update our concept of theory of relativity by better models or other models. Wrong knowledge will be taking Newton's theory of gravitation : and we will pay the price for it when we try to calculate the motion of planets.
Right/Wrong knowledge has it's consequences....On a related note: Neitschze is correct that there is no one size fits all concept of right and wrong. But at the same time, this does not mean there is no right or wrong at all. For that matter, some moral principles are common to cultures, species, and some are universal.

